  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM EDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nToday-Tonight...Mid-level ridging persists across the Florida \npeninsula today, with the Atlantic high and its ridge axis \nremaining draped across central Florida. This will lead to \ncontinued light and variable winds through this morning, becoming \nmore southerly to southeasterly as the east coast sea breeze \ndevelops this afternoon and pushes inland. While drier air aloft \nfrom Saharan dust drifting across the area will help limit rain \nand storm chances across east central Florida today, isolated \nshowers and storms near the greater Orlando area cannot be ruled \nout. Sufficient daytime heating paired with the dry air aloft will\nlead to a conditionally favorable environment for storm \ndevelopment and any storms that do manage to develop will be \ncapable of producing frequent lightning strikes, wind gusts up to \n55 mph, and brief, heavy downpours. Weak steering flow will lead \nto slow-moving and even erratic storm motions, so some quick \nrainfall accumulations of 1-3\" cannot be ruled out in some spots \nthough confidence in this remains low. Activity will diminish by \nthis evening, with dry conditions forecast through the overnight \nhours areawide.\n\nHeat remains a concern across east central Florida today, with \nafternoon temperatures forecast to climb into the mid to upper 90s\nareawide. Some locations may even near their record highs. These \nhot temperatures will combine with humid conditions to produce \npeak heat indices of 108F to 112F, with a Heat Advisory once again\nin effect for all east central Florida counties between 10 AM and\n8 PM. Prolonged heat increases the risk of developing heat-\nrelated illness for everyone, so be sure to practice heat safety. \nIf spending extended periods of time outdoors, stay well hydrated,\nwear lightweight, lightly colored clothing, and take frequent \nbreaks in the shade or an air conditioned building. Never leave \nchildren or pets alone in cars and be sure to look before you \nlock. Limited relief is anticipated through the overnight hours, \nwith lows falling only into the mid to upper 70s.\n\nMonday-Saturday...Ridging aloft weakens through this week as a \ntrough across the eastern US gradually strengthens and digs \nsouthward. At the surface, the Atlantic high shifts southward as \nan area of low pressure moves offshore the Mid-Atlantic coast, \ndragging a cold front southward across the eastern US. The front \nis anticipated to stall and eventually wash out near to just north\nof Florida, with a plume of moisture along and ahead of the \nboundary pushing southward towards the Florida peninsula. PWATs \nincrease to 2\" and greater across northern portions of east \ncentral Florida on Monday, expanding areawide from Tuesday onward.\nThis increasing moisture will support a return to a diurnal \npattern with near-normal rain chances across east central Florida,\nwith PoPs generally ranging between 20-50% on Monday and 40-70% \nfrom Tuesday onward. Storms will be possible with this activity \nwith lightning strikes, gusty winds, and heavy downpours the \nprimary storm hazards. Activity will generally push offshore \nthanks to westerly winds aloft, with conditions remaining mostly \ndry through the overnight hours across the peninsula.\n\nThe heat is anticipated to persist into Monday across east \ncentral Florida, with highs in the mid to upper 90s. Tuesday \nonward, afternoon highs begin a slow trend downward, but values \nare still forecast to remain slightly above normal for this time \nof year. Peak heat indices exceeding 100F will persist each day \nthough this upcoming week, with some guidance suggesting nearing \nand/or exceeding Heat Advisory criteria through mid-week. This \nwill continue to closely be monitored and the proper heat products\nwill be issued as needed. Residents and visitors are encouraged \nto continue practicing heat safety if spending extended periods of\ntime outdoors.",
